# 1
S += [title('CHAPTER 1\nINTRODUCTION'), h('1.1 Background'), P('Contracts govern confidential information, rent, employment, service delivery, payment, liability and termination. They are often written as lengthy documents whose interpretation depends on text spread across multiple locations. A confidentiality clause may refer to a definition, a list of exceptions, an annexure, and a survival clause. A useful engineering system must therefore preserve both semantic meaning and document structure.', 'Body'), P('Recent legal NLP research has progressed from isolated clause classification to document-level inference and retrieval-augmented generation. Yet a major practical issue remains: a system can retrieve a clause related to a question but fail to retrieve a cited dependency, then generate a polished response anyway. The proposed platform treats this situation as an evidence-completeness failure rather than a prompt-writing problem.', 'Body'), h('1.2 Problem Definition'), P('The problem is to answer questions over an uploaded agreement while making the supporting evidence inspectable and while refusing to infer information that is not contained in the document. In addition, the system should surface common review risks and draft controlled negotiation alternatives without presenting them as legal determinations.', 'Body'), h('1.3 Objectives'), grid([['Objective','Measurable project outcome'],['O1','Parse digital and scanned PDFs into page-linked clause units.'],['O2','Retrieve semantically relevant clauses using a persistent ChromaDB collection.'],['O3','Expand evidence through explicit cross-reference links in a Contract Graph.'],['O4','Block or qualify answers when a required dependency is unresolved.'],['O5','Generate citations restricted to retrieved evidence.'],['O6','Support policy-guided risk review and human-review redline alternatives.']], [3*cm,13.5*cm]), h('1.4 Scope and Limitations'), bullets(['Single document workspace with four representative agreement categories.', 'Evidence-backed answers, review findings and controlled redline suggestions.', 'No determination of legal validity, enforceability, jurisdictional compliance or legal outcome.', 'Not a replacement for an advocate, law firm, HR professional or contract manager.', 'Policy checks are curated prototype rules; they are not a complete legal knowledge base.']), h('1.5 Social, Ethical and Environmental Considerations'), P('The platform reduces time spent locating provisions but should not be used to make high-impact decisions without review. The user interface explicitly labels answers as document-grounded, includes evidence citations and records missing-evidence conditions. The smallest suitable model and bounded context are used to avoid unnecessary cloud computation. Uploaded documents may contain personal or commercial information; a production deployment would require access control, encryption, retention controls and deletion workflows.', 'Body'), PageBreak()]

# 2
S += [title('CHAPTER 2\nLITERATURE REVIEW AND RESEARCH GAP'), h('2.1 Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive Tasks'), P('RAG combines a retriever with a generator so answers can be grounded in external documents rather than relying only on model parameters. Lewis et al. [1] established the general approach. In legal work, the requirement is stricter: the model must preserve document provenance and should not invent a missing exception or attachment.', 'Body'), h('2.2 Legal Contract Understanding'), P('ContractNLI [2] evaluates document-level natural-language inference in contracts and highlights evidence spans, negation and exceptions. The CUAD dataset [3] supports clause-focused contract understanding, but it does not itself solve multi-clause evidence assembly. LegalBench [4] broadens evaluation across legal reasoning tasks. These works motivate clear evaluation rather than an unmeasured claim of “legal intelligence”.', 'Body'), h('2.3 Legal RAG and Graph-based Reasoning'), P('LegalBench-RAG [5] studies retrieval-augmented legal question answering. PAKTON [6] evaluates multi-agent retrieval for long legal agreements. COMPACT [7] uses clause graphs for compliance reasoning. These directions show why relation-aware retrieval is important, but they also leave a practical challenge: the presence of a reference must be distinguished from the availability of the referenced material.', 'Body'), h('2.4 Comparative Review and Gap'), grid([['Work','Strength','Unaddressed gap relevant to this project'],['RAG [1]','Grounds generation in retrieved passages','Does not guarantee that document dependencies are complete.'],['ContractNLI [2]','Evidence-aware contract inference','Focuses on NLI, not end-user RAG safety workflow.'],['LegalBench-RAG [5]','Legal retrieval benchmark','Does not itself supply a dependency-completeness decision layer.'],['PAKTON [6]','Long-agreement RAG framework','Complex orchestration is not a transparent prototype guardrail.'],['COMPACT [7]','Clause graph compliance reasoning','Graph connectivity does not alone prove supporting attachment availability.']], [3.0*cm,5.0*cm,8.5*cm]), h('2.5 Proposed Research Gap'), P('<b>Research gap:</b> Existing systems can retrieve legal clauses and some can reason over cross-references, but a relevant clause is not necessarily sufficient evidence. If an answer depends on an absent schedule, exhibit, definition or exception, a model may still provide unsupported text. This project proposes a dependency-completeness gate that explicitly checks whether selected evidence paths terminate in available contract material before generation.', 'Quote'), h('2.6 Research Hypothesis'), P('H1: Adding Contract Graph expansion improves evidence completeness for questions whose answer depends on explicit internal references. H2: Adding a dependency-completeness gate lowers the unsupported-answer rate on deliberately incomplete contracts, with a measurable trade-off in abstention rate.', 'Body'), PageBreak()]

# 3
S += [title('CHAPTER 3\nSYSTEM DESIGN AND METHODOLOGY'), h('3.1 Design Considerations'), P('The system is designed for traceability, modularity and safe degradation. It separates parsing, retrieval, relationship reasoning, policy checks and language generation so that a failure can be identified at a specific layer. The interface shows the relevant clauses, page numbers, direct versus linked evidence, and a safety decision.', 'Body')]
S += fig_flow('Figure 3.1: End-to-end evidence-first architecture.', [('Upload','PDF / sample agreement'),('Parser','Text, pages, clauses, refs'),('ChromaDB','Semantic clause index'),('Contract Graph','Reference dependency edges'),('Guardrails','Completeness + answer support'),('Groq LLM','Cited response / abstention')])
S += [h('3.2 Document Ingestion and Clause Construction'), P('The FastAPI parser uses PyMuPDF to extract page-linked text. A structural segmenter detects headings and clause-like units, assigns stable identifiers, preserves page number and source text, and extracts references such as “Section 4.1”, “Clause 7” and “Schedule A”. When embedded text is too sparse, a local macOS Vision OCR route is used for scanned documents. The original PDF is retained as the source artifact; OCR output is only an extraction aid.', 'Body'), h('3.3 Vector Retrieval and ChromaDB'), P('Each clause is stored as a document with metadata: document ID, clause ID, heading, page, type and source location. A sentence-transformer embedding model converts the clause text to a vector. The user question is embedded using the same embedding model, and ChromaDB returns semantically close candidate clauses. The generation model does not need to produce the same embeddings: embedding search and answer generation are different tasks.', 'Body'), h('3.4 Contract Graph and Policy Graph'), P('The Contract Graph represents explicit relationships between contract clauses and referenced attachments. It is optimized for explainable graph traversal: each edge includes source, target and relation type. The Policy Graph is separate. It stores versioned review checks and approved drafting patterns for a specific agreement type. This separation prevents a policy rule from being misrepresented as text found in the uploaded agreement.', 'Body')]
S += fig_flow('Figure 3.2: Retrieval, graph expansion and guardrail path.', [('Question','Natural language input'),('Candidate clauses','Hybrid semantic + lexical'),('Graph expansion','Linked definitions / schedules'),('Evidence packet','Deduplicated source text'),('Decision','Answer, clarify or abstain')])
S += [h('3.5 Technology Selection'), grid([['Technology','Selected role','Reason'],['React + TypeScript','Web client','Component-based accessible interface and typed state.'],['Node.js + Express','Orchestration API','Coordinates upload, retrieval, graph logic and LLM calls.'],['FastAPI + PyMuPDF','Document parsing','Python ecosystem supports reliable PDF page processing.'],['ChromaDB','Vector store','Persistent local semantic index with metadata filters.'],['Groq LLM API','Generation','Low-latency model inference for bounded evidence prompts.'],['PostgreSQL (planned production)','Transactional metadata/audit store','Suited for users, documents, audit trail and relational relationships.']], [3.1*cm,5.0*cm,8.4*cm]), h('3.6 Data Flow and Privacy'), P('A document is uploaded to the platform, validated for PDF type and size, parsed into clauses, indexed locally, and queried through an evidence pipeline. Only the selected source clauses and question are sent to the generation provider in live mode. Secrets remain on the server. A production release would add authenticated workspaces, at-rest encryption, per-user access control, retention limits and a delete/export workflow.', 'Body'), PageBreak()]

