S += [title('CHAPTER 5\nTESTING, RESULTS AND DISCUSSION'), h('5.1 Experimental Setup'), P('The prototype was exercised using four representative agreement types: Mutual NDA, Residential Rent Agreement, Employment Agreement and Software Service Agreement. Both generated born-digital PDFs and an uploaded scanned rent agreement were used. Tests covered parser output, persistence, live generation, conversational follow-up, missing-evidence conditions, risk review, controlled redlining and errors.', 'Body'), h('5.2 Acceptance Tests'), grid([['ID','Test / input','Expected result','Status'],['TC-01','Upload a valid born-digital agreement PDF','Clauses, pages and references are indexed.','Pass'],['TC-02','Upload a scanned rent agreement','OCR fallback produces searchable clauses.','Pass'],['TC-03','Ask a supported NDA exclusion question','Cited answer uses supporting exclusion clause.','Pass'],['TC-04','Ask a question requiring a missing schedule','Specific insufficient-evidence reason; no invented content.','Pass'],['TC-05','Ask a vague follow-up: “What do I do with it?”','Clarification or prior-answer context handling.','Pass'],['TC-06','Run residential deposit review','Risk finding cites applicable clause and policy rationale.','Pass'],['TC-07','Draft an NDA clause change','Original, controlled alternative and human-review label shown.','Pass'],['TC-08','Invalid/non-PDF upload','Actionable validation error; no partial index.','Pass'],['TC-09','Citation outside retrieved evidence','Verifier rejects or flags unsupported citation.','Pass'],['TC-10','Document library refresh after upload','Persisted document and clause count displayed.','Pass']], [1.3*cm,4.2*cm,7.4*cm,2.1*cm]), h('5.3 Observed Behaviour'), P('The system succeeds when an answer is supported by one or more indexed clauses and any required dependencies can be resolved. On a rent agreement query about security-deposit deductions, it retrieves the deposit clause and returns a textual explanation with the corresponding clause identifier. On a question that requires an absent Schedule B, the dependency gate identifies the unresolved reference and returns a structured evidence-missing result. This difference is the key behavioural claim of the project.', 'Body'), h('5.4 Contract Review Snapshot'), grid([['Indicator','Meaning','Interpretation'],['Evidence completeness','Whether referenced supporting clauses/schedules are available','Higher is better; it is not legal validity.'],['Clause clarity','Specificity and ambiguity indicators in selected clauses','Lower clarity triggers review, not a legal conclusion.'],['Risk flags','Count/severity of policy-backed findings','A prompt for human attention.'],['Negotiation readiness','Whether a reviewed drafting pattern is available','A readiness signal, not automatic approval.']], [3.5*cm,7*cm,6*cm]), h('5.5 Discussion'), P('The most important result is qualitative: the platform exposes why it answered or stopped. A conventional chat interface may make the same answer appear equally reliable whether it was supported by direct text or inferred from missing content. By contrast, the evidence trace makes direct clauses, dependency hops and guardrail decisions visible. This allows reviewers to inspect errors at the retrieval or evidence stage rather than treating the LLM as an opaque authority.', 'Body'), h('5.6 Threats to Validity'), bullets(['The current test set is representative but not a statistically large corpus.', 'Clause segmentation and OCR can be degraded by unusual layouts or low-quality scans.', 'Policy-graph rules are curated and limited; they do not cover every jurisdiction or contract type.', 'LLM latency, provider changes and model behaviour are external dependencies.', 'No legal-expert study has yet measured professional usefulness or legal correctness.']), PageBreak()]

# 6
S += [title('CHAPTER 6\nCONCLUSION, LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK'), h('6.1 Achievements'), P('The project demonstrates a functioning evidence-first legal document intelligence prototype. It supports PDF ingestion, page-linked clause construction, semantic indexing in ChromaDB, explicit reference graphs, grounded Groq answers with citations, evidence-completeness checks, risk review, controlled redline drafting, an Evidence Trace panel and four demonstrable agreement categories. The work connects these components into a single inspectable workflow rather than presenting a generic chatbot.', 'Body'), h('6.2 Research Contribution'), P('The project’s contribution is the dependency-completeness gate. It operationalizes an important distinction for legal RAG: retrieval relevance is not sufficient evidence quality. When a selected clause requires a missing schedule or definition, the system identifies the dependency and abstains or qualifies its answer. This creates a clear hypothesis for future measured evaluation against baseline RAG.', 'Body'), h('6.3 Limitations'), P('The platform does not determine legal validity, enforceability or statutory compliance. It does not replace legal advice. The current graph is based primarily on explicit textual references; implicit relationships remain difficult. Multi-document amendment precedence, access control, audited policy sources and expert evaluation are future work. The OCR fallback is useful for scanned files but needs broader robustness testing.', 'Body'), h('6.4 Future Work'), bullets(['Create a benchmark of contract questions labelled with direct evidence, linked evidence and required dependencies.', 'Compare baseline RAG, hybrid retrieval, Contract Graph RAG and Contract Graph RAG plus completeness gate using answer-support and abstention metrics.', 'Add authoritative, versioned legal-policy source packs as a separate corpus—never mix them with contract text without provenance labels.', 'Support amendments, statements of work and supersession relations across multiple contract versions.', 'Add user accounts, PostgreSQL audit records, encryption and configurable retention/deletion policies.', 'Conduct usability and expert-review studies with legal professionals.']), h('6.5 Societal and Ethical Statement'), P('The platform is designed to improve access to document evidence, not to automate legal judgement. Its human-review notices, citation design and abstention mechanisms intentionally expose uncertainty. These principles are especially important where contracts affect housing, employment, privacy, payment and commercial relationships.', 'Body'), PageBreak()]

